  "body": "Stacked on #1144 (on #1139, #1123, #560, #551) - this diff shows their commits until they merge; merge #551, #560, #1123, #1139, #1144 first. Fork synced at v0.18.0.\n\n## What changed\n\nThe Wire Contour op (#532) now cuts **one open run entered from the stock's edge** - a cut-off line, a slot, a relief - besides one closed profile (part of #290).\n\n- **Kerf side stated, never inferred:** `side` gains `left` / `right` (of the run's travel = its point order) beside `die` / `punch`. Die/punch on an open run is refused by name (\"an open profile has no punch or die side; state the kerf side: left or right of the run's travel\"); left/right on a closed profile likewise. Still no serde default for `side`.\n- **No start hole:** both ends must reach (or pass) the stock's edge (1 um tolerance). The path is the run's one-sided parallel at wire radius + gap (mitred joins), extended along the run's own end directions until the wire stands **kerf offset + a stated `clearance`** off the stock: threaded there in the air, run out the same way at the far end (no return).\n- **Refused by name:** no stock, an end that stops inside the stock (with its distance), a run that never enters the stock, a self-crossing run, one that doubles back, several runs or a run mixed with rings, an inside corner tighter than wire radius + gap (cover law, same 2 um as #532), a run-out that would cut the stock, a bad clearance.\n- The kerf law and the cover law now run over an open polyline too (`laws::cover_run`, `kerf(.., given_closed, ..)`); the closed path is byte-identical (both #532 goldens unchanged).\n- App: `WireContourOp.clearance` (seeded 1.0 mm; the file omits it at the seed, so closed-cut files read/write as before), `is_sane` bounds it, the generator gets the setup's stock outline (stock facets projected to XY, read only when a run is picked). The existing MCP door (`params_from_json` / the edit patch) carries `side: left|right` and `clearance` - no new MCP tool (#1108 is the dedicated one).\n- UI: the minimal wire page offers the four sides and a \"Run-out clearance\" row (#535 owns the real page).\n\nDecisions: `docs/decisions/1109-wire-open-profile.md` (side in one field, travel = point order, no centred kerf yet, no blind runs - a run ending inside the material is a closed cut from a start hole, run-out shape, clearance seed).\n\n## Readings\n\nPlate 100 x 50, synthetic condition C110 (wire 0.25, gap 0.04, offset 0.165), clearance 1.0. Cut-off at x = 60, kerf right: every wire point at x = 60.165 (1e-9); threaded at Y-1.165, run out at Y51.165. Left: x = 59.835 - the two stand apart by the 0.33 kerf. A U slot from the edge (2 mm corners, kerf left, inside the U): every ride point exactly 0.165 off the run (1e-6) and inside the slot; the same U with sharp corners is refused (\"minimum inside radius is 0.1650 mm\") on the left and cut (mitred) on the right.\n\n## Integration with #1144 (wire-iso post #1139 + wire verify #534)\n\n`issue/534-wire-verify` is merged into this branch (`5c49075a`, no textual conflicts) and the open run is joined to both (`0d82d2e7`):\n\n- **Verify** (`sim::verify::wire`): a third side `CutSide::Open` for `left|right`. The kerf law judges the run, run-in and run-out included, against the part at every judged height, so an open run whose kerf touches the part is a gouge and blocks the post. The start-hole/threading and slug laws are skipped, and the report line says so: \"Cutoff1 open run (right of travel): kerf judged; start-hole and slug laws not applied (...)\". Both ends, where the wire is threaded and cut, must stand clear of the stock by the kerf offset at every height (`OpenEndInStock`). The app gate maps `left|right` to `Open` and no longer refuses an open run for having no start hole.\n- **Post** (`wire-iso.cps`): it threads at the path's first point (the run-in, in the air) and cuts the wire at section end after the run-out. It writes only the path's moves, so no return to the threading point exists. Without AWT the operator notes read \"at the run-in point outside the stock\" and \"at the run-out point\". The closed punch golden is byte-identical.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/1109-wire-open-profile.md` now covers how verify and the post read an open run.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `camcraft-motion` lib `wire_edm::open_tests` (3): `a_cut_off_across_a_plate_stands_the_kerf_offset_on_the_stated_side`, `a_slot_from_the_edge_rides_inside_at_the_kerf_offset`, `every_open_run_refusal_is_named`; #532's 5 `wire_edm::tests` unchanged and green.\n- `golden_cl`: new `wire_open_cutoff_matches_golden` (`tests/golden/wire_open_cutoff.cl`); `wire_contour_die_and_punch_match_golden` unchanged - **no golden moved**.\n- `camcraft-app` `wire_contour`: new `the_open_run_fields_ride_the_commands_and_the_json_door` (EditOperationParams + undo, NaN clearance refused at the door, JSON door both ways, a closed file names no clearance, left/right on the closed slug refused at generation).\n- Integration: `wire_post::an_open_cutoff_posts_its_golden_and_a_gouging_one_is_refused` (new NC golden `tests/golden/nc/wire-iso/wire_contour_open_cutoff.nc`, synthetic fixture dialect). A 60x50x12 part in a 100x50x12 bar, its x=60 top edge picked as an open chain: it generates through the commands, is judged, and posts with one G0 to the run-in at Y-1.165, cuts to Y51.165, then FX-CUT with no return. The same run with its kerf on the part's side is refused at the post (`Cutoff1: wire gouge`) and no NC is written. The punch golden is asserted byte-identical. Also `sim` `verify::wire::tests::an_open_run_is_judged_by_its_kerf_with_no_start_hole_or_slug` (clean pass with no slug and the skip said; a gouge; an end in the stock).\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/issue/534-wire-verify` on the final head `0d82d2e7`: **green**, 13.7 min (sim 3 s, motion lib 27 s, app 676 s: every suite because a shared golden changed, wire_post/wire_contour/wire_verify included; ui 115 s). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-motion -p camcraft-sim -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.\n- Earlier: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/issue/532-wire-contour` on `42bee8fa`: **green** - motion lib (33 s), app (the whole suite, because a golden was added; 645 s), ui (lib and 7 suites). The first clippy run flagged one needless-range-loop in `open.rs` (fixed in `42bee8fa`); `cargo clippy -p camcraft-motion -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean on the final head.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- **Not exercised on a real EDM control**; the condition and the post's EDM codes are synthetic.\n- The stock read in `generate.rs` is now exercised end to end by the integration test above. A slot (two edge-reaching ends on one side) is pinned only at the generator.\n- The capture/tab law for the offcut a cut-off frees is not judged: no slug law runs for an open run (#539 owns tabs).\n- The page's new rows were drawn only by the registry-wide tab law test.\n\nChangelog: Wire EDM: the Wire Contour cuts an open run (a cut-off or a slot) entered from the stock's edge, with the kerf stated left or right of travel and the wire threaded and run out a stated clearance off the stock; an end inside the stock, a missing kerf side, a self-crossing run and a too-tight corner are refused by name (#1109)\n\nEffort: high (not escalated)\n\nFixes #1109\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
